    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "fba13641-4559-3885-a2a7-4951d334fce2",
                "word": "Phoebus",
                "pos": "name",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "3d845f1e-1de5-36cf-802a-d666295ac2e3",
                        "entry_id": "fba13641-4559-3885-a2a7-4951d334fce2",
                        "glosses": [
                            "An epithet and synonym for Helios; Phoebus Helios, after his maternal aunt, Phoebe."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Greek"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "35b672a3-9d5f-4c1a-8126-507d87ee10ea",
                                "sense_id": "3d845f1e-1de5-36cf-802a-d666295ac2e3",
                                "text": "Mor. Miſlike me not for my complexion,\nThe ſhadowed liuerie of the burniſht ſunne,\nTo whom I am a neighbour, and neere bred.\nBring me the faireſt creature North-ward borne,\nWhere Phœbus fire ſcarce thawes the yſicles,\nAnd let vs make inciſion for your loue,\nTo proue whoſe blood is reddeſt, his or mine."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "3ef2b00c-0675-3f12-a620-6d39246abf13",
                        "entry_id": "fba13641-4559-3885-a2a7-4951d334fce2",
                        "glosses": [
                            "An epithet and synonym for Apollo; Phoebus Apollo."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Greek",
                            "Roman"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "17ebd349-0d92-4d7e-9124-4f284221b328",
                                "sense_id": "3ef2b00c-0675-3f12-a620-6d39246abf13",
                                "text": "By Phœbus, here's a moſt neate fine ſtreete; is't not? I proteſt to thee, I am enamord of this ſtreete now, more then of halfe the ſtreetes of Rome, againe; tis ſo polite, and terſe; […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "fac19623-f39a-4915-8b09-f3fe3f474933",
                                "sense_id": "3ef2b00c-0675-3f12-a620-6d39246abf13",
                                "text": "The heaven doth not contain so many stars,\nSo many leaves not prostrate lie in woods,\nWhen autumn’s old and Boreas sounds his wars,\nSo many waves have not the ocean floods,\nAs my rent mind hath torments all the night,"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "644be0c8-ebc0-422e-b0c3-300e7c2e61f0",
                                "sense_id": "3ef2b00c-0675-3f12-a620-6d39246abf13",
                                "text": "For she recalled the edict which decreed\nIf in one day she did not take or kill\nThe challenger (to this King Charles agreed),\nShe was his captive and must do his will.\nPhoebus was soon to plunge his golden head\nInto the western sea when of her skill\nAnd strength the Maid at last began doubt,\nWhile her fatigue was putting hope to rout.[…]"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "75047bb8-e0cd-4f5d-83e9-e8517bba1a3f",
                        "source_entry_id": "fba13641-4559-3885-a2a7-4951d334fce2",
                        "source_sense_description": "divine epithet",
                        "target_lang_word": "Phébus",
